the traditional process of brick making  in afghanistan has been in place for  hundreds of years  clay is turned to mud which is then  hardened and then molded into bricks  yasa should be in school but he's in  this factory with his brothers and  cousins because his family of 12 needs  him to work  the u.n says 88 of families in the  country have at least one child between  the ages of 11 and 17 working in  difficult conditions  yasir says he has little choice as his  family's quality of life has worsened  rapidly  in the past year our lives have changed  now we can't buy clothes for our  families we cannot even eat proper food  there are sixteen hundred brick making  factories in the day subs area of kabul  alone  but the country's economic crisis means  there aren't many buyers for their  product  as coal becomes expensive the owners  here do not raise the price of the  bricks but rather cut down the wages the  workforce most of it coming from eastern  afghanistan only works during the summer  and struggles to find jobs through the  rest of the year  the workers get about three dollars to  make a thousand bricks although fewer  kilns are operating now  i worked for three months on a dairy  farm in nangarhar but a disease outbreak  ended the work now i've moved here to  make bricks  the taliban which calls itself the  islamic camera it says it understands  the problem but the options are child  labor or starvation  if the islamic emirate of afghanistan  would have the power and capacity we  would never let any child work but the  problem is that first we are in a bad  economic situation and secondly the  families of these children are poor and  helpless  the taliban says it's trying to revive  the economy and it's appealing for  international help  one person from one family can feed  around 20 people if they are employed if  countries want to help use the human  resource of afghanistan provide  employment opportunities  but until such options can be found many  afghan children and adults like these  will have to go on baking bricks to  survive swamiji al jazeera kabul
 

